  "file": "src/util/proc.ts",
  "line": 57,
  "title": "An unreadable ancestor leaves higher ancestors selectable",
  "detail": "The ancestry walk in src/util/proc.ts:57 and src/util/processes-darwin.ts:125 stops at an ancestor it can't read, but discovery then continues with the incomplete exclusion set. An unreadable parent can have a readable, marked ancestor (for example a test runner or daemon carrying the invocation marker), which cleanup will select and signal. A probe feeding both real scanners synthetic inspection data (an unreadable parent with a marked grandparent) returned that grandparent as a selected descendant. Fail closed on incomplete ancestry (refuse to select, or skip signalling, when the caller's ancestry can't be established) rather than relying on the partial set, and add a regression for both the Linux and Darwin scanners with an unreadable parent and a marked higher ancestor."
 },
 {
  "id": "F2",
